WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.650
Today we stand on the threshold of a new golden
age of space exploration.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.100
The first of three missions that will see
humans return to the moon for the first time

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.750
in 5 decades.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:18.760
The SLS, or Space Launch System, today rests
on the stand, waiting to ignite its engines,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.690
taking the Orion capsule on a journey around
the moon.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:28.699
This is an exciting launch for any true aerospace
enthusiast, but legitimate questions about

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:31.260
the SLS have been raised.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.230
Questions about its very existence.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.800
In an era of innovation that has allowed the
private space industry to drastically reduce

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.480
the costs of launch.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.180
Why is NASA building its own, completely un-reusable,
rocket?

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.260
Why has it cost so much money to manufacture?

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.160
And why does it exist at all?

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:54.630
The SLS is a massive rocket, building on not
just the legacy of the Saturn program, but

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.460
the Space Shuttle Program.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.180
Borrowing concepts and actual parts from each
system.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.300
The SLS consists of 2 solid rocket boosters.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.819
Together they will produce more than 75 percent
of the rocket’s thrust at launch, burning

00:01:08.819 --> 00:01:14.479
five tonnes of propellant every 6 seconds
and providing the equivalent thrust of 14

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:17.749
- 4 engined 747 jumbo jets…each.

00:01:17.749 --> 00:01:18.840
[1]

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.929
These boosters are essentially the same boosters
that flew on the Space Shuttle, in fact they

00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:28.689
are using many leftover parts from the space
shuttle program.

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:34.069
With some updated designs to the nozzle and
an additional 5th booster segment, giving

00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:40.039
this new generation booster 25% more fuel
capacity over the Shuttles.

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:44.749
However, unlike the solid rocket boosters
of the Space Shuttle, these boosters will

00:01:44.749 --> 00:01:47.099
not be recovered and refurbished.

00:01:47.099 --> 00:01:52.090
In an era where we can literally fly our boosters
back to the ground, this seems like a strange

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:53.229
choice.

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:55.850
Why did NASA decide to go this route?

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:58.719
To understand that we have to look back in
time.

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:01.969
Two programs were canceled in 2011.

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:05.509
The Space Shuttle program and the Constellation
Program.

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:09.519
We are all familiar with the Space Shuttle
Program, but the constellation program is

00:02:09.519 --> 00:02:14.040
less known, due to the fact it only ever flew
a prototype rocket.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:19.560
The Ares prototype flew in 2009 and tested
the Orion launch abort system.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.019
If that name sounds familiar, that’s because
Orion is the same spacecraft that will be

00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:26.500
flying on top of the SLS.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:33.200
The Orion spacecraft was originally designed
for the constellation program before its cancellation

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.200
in 2011.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.109
In fact, if we look at the final planned Ares
rocket design, the Ares V, it bears a striking

00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:41.950
resemblance to the SLS.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:47.120
That’s no coincidence, just as parts from
the Space Shuttle program were scavenged for

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.989
the SLS, designs and employees were taken
from the Constellation program.

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:55.689
We see this pattern play out throughout the
SLS’s design.

00:02:55.689 --> 00:03:01.730
The giant orange insulation covered core stage,
containing two gigantic propellant tanks holding

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:07.730
2 million liters of liquid hydrogen and 742
thousand liters of liquid oxygen, will be

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:11.090
feeding 4 RS-25 engines.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.769
These engines are not only the same design
used for the Space Shuttle, but they are literally

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:19.140
scavenged from previously flown Space Shuttles.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.290
The four engines for Artemis 1 have these
serial numbers, and from that we can track

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:25.790
their flight history.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:31.349
These engines have a rich history within NASA,
bringing components of the ISS to orbit and

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:34.790
flying astronauts to Hubble to repair and
maintain it.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:35.790
[1]

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.811
These are tried and tested engines that in
theory would save NASA money, but oddly, the

00:03:40.811 --> 00:03:44.579
SLS program's greatest criticism is its cost.

00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:48.239
Running up a bill of 23 billion dollars.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:54.099
When Barack Obama released the US government's
budget in 2010, the constellation budget showed

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:55.189
all zeros.

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:57.760
The program was effectively canceled.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.840
Obama’s vision for NASA’s future was for
collaboration between commercial space partners

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:08.940
like SpaceX, while shifting NASA’s budget
and focus to projects which were not commercially

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:09.940
viable.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:10.940
[2]

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.739
A vision that is largely coming to fruition
today.

00:04:12.739 --> 00:04:19.440
So, if the Constellation program was canceled
in 2010, how is this resurrected frankenstein's

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.360
monster standing on the pad today, ready to
launch to the moon?

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:25.430
The answer.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:26.430
Politics.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.300
“This legislation will define NASA’s future
by building on its past”

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:36.560
Obama’s cancellation of the Constellation
program was met with criticism from politicians

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.460
from all over the United States.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:43.580
The elevated costs of the program was partially
by design.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:50.319
NASA gains support in congress by spreading
jobs all across the United States, ensuring

00:04:50.319 --> 00:04:55.500
widespread support by the constituents of
the politicians in each region.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.750
When the constellation program was canceled
it left thousands of people without work.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.569
People were understandably upset.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:08.600
This forced the Obama administration to back
peddle, resulting in the NASA Authorisation

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.800
Act of 2010 being signed.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:17.070
Providing NASA budget to both the commercial
space launch industry, and in effect, brought

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:21.100
the constellation program back from the dead
under a new name.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.190
Artemis

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:28.699
The act even specified that NASA needed to
reuse technology, contracts and workforce

00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:30.240
from the constellation program.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.240
[3]

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:36.580
And so, here we are, 12 years later, with
the ghost of constellation flying to the moon,

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.930
itself a Frankenstein's monster of space shuttle
components.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:46.790
This is clearly not an efficient way of developing
a rocket, canceling one over budget project

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:51.610
and then bringing it back a couple months
later with added design restrictions.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:55.630
That’s perfectly obvious to anyone and everyone
involved.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:06:00.659
It’s just how bureaucracy in a democratic
system shakes out sometimes.

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:03.879
Could that money have been spent better elsewhere?

00:06:03.879 --> 00:06:04.879
Absolutely.

00:06:04.879 --> 00:06:08.970
Would it have been spent in NASA without the
intentional cost inefficiencies?

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.440
Probably not.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:16.060
This was a jobs program where we get an incredible
rocket as the output.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:20.770
The SLS, despite its inefficiencies, is undeniably
cool.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.620
The sound from solid rocket boosters is something
I have dreamt of experiencing.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:29.890
I don’t think anyone with an honest love
of aerospace can look at this machine and

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.160
not be in awe.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:35.661
It looks like someone asked midjourney to
create a hybrid of the Saturn V and Space

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:36.661
Shuttle.

00:06:36.661 --> 00:06:41.620
And perhaps that’s nostalgia for an era
I was born too late to experience, but that

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.810
era took us to the moon.

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:47.300
I have a tattoo of Buzz Aldrin on the moon
on my leg.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.539
To say I’m excited is an understatement.

00:06:50.539 --> 00:06:55.330
This machine, if all goes to plan, is going
to take us back to the moon.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:07:00.419
With the progress happening in the commercial
space industry following hot on NASA’s heels,

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:06.100
we will have cheaper heavy lift rockets capable
of taking us to the Moon and further, NASA

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.740
won’t need to funnel money into a project
like this again.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:13.889
Instead being able to focus on projects like
the James Webb Telescope.

00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:19.270
This is an exciting time in aerospace and
human space flight after a lull of more than

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.100
50 years.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.720
Times are changing.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:29.550
This is a stepping stone to a permanent human
habitat on the moon, and maybe someday, Mars.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:34.500
I grew up watching documentaries about the
Moon missions, always with a little bit of

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:40.880
